# Vascular Pathology (Gardner) — Image Quiz V2, 18 items, every stem has an image. NBME/NBOME item-writer spec.
Q = [
dict(img='Vhyal', cap='Photomicrograph of an arteriole, kidney biopsy specimen (H&E)',
stem="A 71-year-old woman comes to the office for follow-up of kidney disease. She has had type 2 diabetes mellitus for 22 years and hypertension for 15 years. Her serum creatinine concentration has risen slowly from 1.2 mg/dL to 1.9 mg/dL over 4 years. Her pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 146/84 mm Hg. Funduscopic examination shows arteriovenous nicking without papilledema. Urinalysis shows 1+ protein and no blood; there are no red blood cell casts. A photomicrograph of an arteriole from a kidney biopsy specimen is shown.{FIG}Which of the following processes is most likely responsible for the change in this arteriolar wall?",
choices=["Leakage of plasma proteins across injured endothelium","Calcium deposition along the internal elastic lamina","Concentric layering of proliferating smooth muscle cells","Immune complex deposition with neutrophil fragmentation","Lipid-laden macrophage accumulation within the intima"], key=0,
exp="A pink, glassy (<b>hyaline</b>), homogeneous thickening of the arteriolar wall with a narrowed lumen = <b>hyaline arteriolosclerosis</b>. Its setting is <b>benign (long-standing, moderate) hypertension, diabetes and ageing</b>. Mechanism: chronic endothelial injury → the barrier leaks → <b>plasma proteins pile up in the wall</b>, and smooth muscle cells (SMCs) add extra matrix → the lumen narrows → in the kidney, <b>benign nephrosclerosis</b> with a slow fall in glomerular filtration. Dr. Gardner: diabetes is \"ultimately a microvascular disease.\"<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hyaline arteriolosclerosis (benign hypertension, diabetes) is caused by plasma protein leakage across injured endothelium plus extra smooth muscle matrix.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for Mönckeberg medial calcific sclerosis: purple calcium in the media of a muscular artery, with the lumen spared.",
2:"Would be correct for hyperplastic (onion-skin) arteriolosclerosis, with blood pressure above about 180/120 mm Hg, papilledema and a rapidly rising creatinine.",
3:"Would be correct for leukocytoclastic (immune-complex) small-vessel vasculitis, with palpable purpura and red blood cell casts.",
4:"Would be correct for atherosclerosis, which affects large and medium arteries, not arterioles."},
eli5="Years of high pressure and high sugar damage the lining of tiny kidney arteries, so blood proteins leak into the wall and stiffen it like glass. The pipe gets narrower and the kidney slowly loses function."),

dict(img='Vonion', cap='Photomicrograph of a kidney biopsy specimen (H&E)',
stem="A 38-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of a 2-day history of severe headache and blurred vision. He was told he had high blood pressure 3 years ago but never took the prescribed medication. His pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 236/144 mm Hg in both arms. Funduscopic examination shows bilateral papilledema with flame-shaped hemorrhages. Serum creatinine concentration is 3.4 mg/dL; it was 1.0 mg/dL 6 months ago. Urinalysis shows 2+ blood and 2+ protein. He has no skin thickening, Raynaud phenomenon, or rash. Antinuclear antibody and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody tests are negative. A photomicrograph of a kidney biopsy specimen is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Benign nephrosclerosis","Malignant hypertension","Microscopic polyangiitis","Polyarteritis nodosa","Scleroderma renal crisis"], key=1,
exp="Concentric, laminated <b>\"onion-skin\"</b> layers of smooth muscle cells with reduplicated basement membrane = <b>hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis</b> (± fibrinoid necrosis = necrotizing arteriolitis). This is the lesion of <b>malignant hypertension</b>: blood pressure around 230/140 mm Hg with end-organ damage (papilledema, a creatinine that tripled in 6 months). Dr. Gardner calls it a <b>medical emergency</b>, now very treatable; pre-eclampsia gives the same lesion. Scleroderma can also produce onion-skinning, which is why the stem removes it (no skin thickening or Raynaud phenomenon, negative antinuclear antibody).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Onion-skin (hyperplastic) arteriolosclerosis with fibrinoid necrosis is the kidney lesion of malignant hypertension.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for hyaline arteriolosclerosis with a slow rise in creatinine over years and moderate blood pressure.",
2:"Would be correct with a positive p-ANCA (antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody against myeloperoxidase), crescents and lung hemorrhage.",
3:"Would be correct with medium-artery fibrinoid necrosis and microaneurysms, often with hepatitis B, rather than onion-skinned arterioles.",
4:"Would be correct with skin thickening, Raynaud phenomenon and a positive antinuclear antibody test."},
eli5="His blood pressure went so high, so fast, that the tiny kidney arteries piled up layer after layer of muscle like an onion. That chokes off the kidney and damages the eyes. It's an emergency."),

dict(img='Vmonck', cap='Photomicrograph of a muscular artery from another patient (H&E)',
stem="An 82-year-old man comes to the office for follow-up 2 weeks after a fall in which he sustained a nondisplaced fracture of the left fifth metatarsal. He walks 2 miles daily without leg pain. The x-ray of the foot obtained after the fall showed incidental parallel linear calcifications outlining the dorsalis pedis artery. His blood pressure is 134/78 mm Hg. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are 2+ bilaterally, and capillary refill is less than 2 seconds. A photomicrograph of a similarly affected artery from another patient is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the absence of ischemic symptoms in this patient?",
choices=["Calcium is deposited in the media, sparing the lumen","Collateral vessels have formed around an occlusion","Nitric oxide release dilates the calcified segment","Lesion confined to arterioles beyond the pulses","A thick fibrous cap protects the plaque from rupture"], key=0,
exp="Purple (calcified) deposits in a ring within the <b>media</b>, along the internal elastic lamina, with an open lumen = <b>Mönckeberg medial calcific sclerosis</b>. Because the calcium sits in the media, <b>the lumen isn't narrowed</b> → usually <b>asymptomatic</b>, an ageing change that shows up on x-ray as \"tram-track\" or \"pipe-stem\" vessels. Dr. Gardner adds that these brittle vessels tear easily with trauma. <i>Board add-on:</i> the stiff arteries resist cuff compression and falsely raise the ankle-brachial index.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Mönckeberg sclerosis calcifies the media and spares the lumen, so it is usually asymptomatic.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for chronic occlusive atherosclerosis, which would have reduced distal pulses or claudication.",
2:"Calcified media can't relax. It is stiff, not dilated. The symptoms are absent because the lumen was never narrowed.",
3:"Would be correct for hyaline arteriolosclerosis, which affects arterioles rather than a named artery visible on x-ray.",
4:"Would be correct for a stable atherosclerotic plaque. That sits in the intima and still narrows the lumen."},
eli5="The calcium is stuck inside the wall of the pipe, not inside the pipe's opening. Blood still flows through normally, so his feet feel fine."),

dict(img='Vfmd', cap='Catheter angiogram (marker)',
stem="A 29-year-old woman comes to the office because her blood pressure has remained about 162/100 mm Hg despite 6 months of treatment with three antihypertensive drugs at maximal doses. She has no history of serious illness and has never had an arterial procedure. She does not smoke and does not use oral contraceptives or stimulants. A bruit is heard in the epigastrium. Radial pulses are equal, and blood pressure is the same in both arms. Serum potassium concentration is 3.3 mEq/L, and plasma renin activity is increased. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 8 mm/h. Angiography of the right renal artery shows the same appearance as the artery shown here.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Fibromuscular intimal hyperplasia","Polyarteritis nodosa","Takayasu arteritis"], key=1,
exp="Alternating narrowings and dilations = a <b>\"string of beads\"</b> → <b>fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD)</b>: a rare, largely inherited condition in which segments of the arterial wall are replaced by connective tissue, with multisite aneurysms. <i>Board add-on:</i> FMD classically hits the <b>renal arteries of young women</b> → renovascular hypertension: low afferent pressure → renin ↑ → angiotensin II ↑ → aldosterone ↑ → vasoconstriction, sodium retention and low potassium. Watch the look-alike names: <b>fibromuscular intimal hyperplasia</b> is the acquired thickening that follows stents, balloons and grafts.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A string-of-beads angiogram in a young woman with resistant hypertension is fibromuscular dysplasia.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct in an older smoker with vascular risk factors and a narrowing at the renal artery origin.",
2:"Would be correct after a stent, balloon angioplasty or vascular graft. She has never had an arterial procedure.",
3:"Would be correct with fever, weight loss, abdominal pain and a raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), often with hepatitis B.",
4:"Would be correct with unequal arm pulses and blood pressures and a raised ESR in a young woman."},
eli5="Parts of her artery wall grew in wrong, so the pipe looks like a string of beads. The kidney senses low flow and pushes her blood pressure up."),

dict(img='Vpanang', cap='Selective visceral angiogram (arrowhead)',
stem="A 52-year-old man comes to the office because of a 3-month history of fever, a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss, and crampy abdominal pain that begins 30 minutes after meals. Two weeks ago he developed weakness of dorsiflexion of the right foot. He injected heroin for 6 years and stopped 15 years ago. His temperature is 38.1°C (100.6°F), pulse is 94/min, and blood pressure is 172/104 mm Hg. There are tender subcutaneous nodules on the left calf and a right foot drop. The lungs are clear to auscultation, and a chest x-ray shows no abnormalities. Serum creatinine concentration is 1.8 mg/dL. Urinalysis shows 1+ protein and no red blood cell casts. The eosinophil count is normal, and tests for antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies are negative. A visceral angiogram is shown.{FIG}Further serologic testing is most likely to show which of the following?",
choices=["Antibodies to double-stranded DNA","Antibodies to glomerular basement membrane","Antibodies to proteinase 3","Cryoglobulins with hepatitis C antibodies","Hepatitis B surface antigen"], key=4,
exp="Multiple <b>microaneurysms</b> and stenoses in medium-sized visceral arteries (the \"rosary\" angiogram) plus postprandial (mesenteric) pain, a foot drop (nerve), new hypertension and a raised creatinine (renal arteries → renin) and skin nodules, with <b>clear lungs</b> and <b>negative ANCA</b> = <b>polyarteritis nodosa (PAN)</b>. Dr. Gardner's table: PAN is medium-vessel, ANCA-negative, immune-complex driven, and linked to <b>hepatitis B</b>. The old injection-drug use is the buried exposure. Histology: transmural fibrinoid necrosis. <i>Board add-on:</i> \"P-A-N = Pulmonary-Avoiding, No ANCA.\"<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Polyarteritis nodosa is an ANCA-negative, lung-sparing, medium-vessel vasculitis with microaneurysms, associated with hepatitis B.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for systemic lupus erythematosus, with rash, arthritis, cytopenias and low complement rather than visceral microaneurysms.",
1:"Would be correct for anti-GBM (glomerular basement membrane) disease, with lung hemorrhage and red blood cell casts.",
2:"Ruled out by the negative ANCA tests. This would fit granulomatosis with polyangiitis with sinus, lung and kidney disease.",
3:"Would be correct for cryoglobulinemic small-vessel vasculitis, with palpable purpura and glomerulonephritis, not medium-artery aneurysms."},
eli5="An old hepatitis B infection set off his immune system against medium-sized arteries. They balloon into tiny bulges and narrow, starving the gut, nerves and kidneys, but the lungs are spared."),

dict(img='Vtemple', cap='Photograph of the right temple',
stem="A 74-year-old man comes to the office because of a 3-week history of headache over the right temple and pain in the jaw that begins after a few minutes of chewing. For 2 months he has had stiffness of the shoulders and hips lasting about an hour each morning. Yesterday he had a 5-minute episode of blurred vision in the right eye that resolved completely. His temperature is 37.6°C (99.7°F), pulse is 82/min, and blood pressure is 138/80 mm Hg. The right temple is shown; the artery is tender and its pulse is diminished. Visual acuity is 20/25 bilaterally, and funduscopic examination shows no abnormalities. No carotid bruits are heard. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 94 mm/h. A temporal artery biopsy can be scheduled in 5 days.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
choices=["Start high-dose prednisone today","Start low-dose aspirin only","Start naproxen and reassess in 1 week","Obtain MRI of the brain first","Wait for the biopsy result to treat","Order carotid duplex ultrasonography"], key=0,
exp="Age over 50, temporal headache, <b>jaw claudication</b>, a thick, tender temporal artery, shoulder/hip stiffness (<b>polymyalgia rheumatica</b>) and a very high erythrocyte sedimentation rate = <b>giant cell (temporal) arteritis</b>. The transient monocular blurring is the warning: ophthalmic artery involvement → <b>irreversible blindness</b>. Dr. Gardner's notes: start <b>high-dose steroids today, before the biopsy result</b>. <i>Board add-on:</i> the biopsy stays diagnostic for days to weeks after steroids start, so treatment doesn't spoil it.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Suspected giant cell arteritis, especially with visual symptoms, is treated with high-dose glucocorticoids immediately, without waiting for the biopsy.",
wrong={1:"Aspirin alone does not control the vasculitis, and the eye is at risk now.",
2:"Would be correct for a musculoskeletal headache with a normal ESR. A nonsteroidal drug does not prevent blindness here.",
3:"Would be correct with a focal neurologic deficit suggesting stroke or a mass. Imaging should not delay steroids.",
4:"Waiting 5 days risks permanent vision loss. Treatment comes first, and the biopsy remains useful afterward.",
5:"Would be correct for amaurosis fugax from carotid atherosclerosis, with a bruit and a normal ESR."},
eli5="An inflamed artery on the side of his head could soon block the artery to his eye. Steroids today protect his sight, and the biopsy can still be done afterward."),

dict(img='Vtaka', cap='Aortic arch angiogram (arrows)',
stem="A 26-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 4-month history of aching in the left arm when she dries her hair and episodes of lightheadedness. Six months ago she had 3 weeks of low-grade fevers, night sweats, and joint aches. She has never smoked. Her blood pressure is 148/88 mm Hg in the right arm and 104/70 mm Hg in the left arm. The left radial pulse is barely palpable. Bruits are heard over both supraclavicular areas. Femoral pulses are strong and are not delayed relative to the right radial pulse. A chest x-ray shows no rib notching. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 58 mm/h. An aortic arch angiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Coarctation of the aorta","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Giant cell arteritis","Subclavian stenosis from atherosclerosis","Takayasu arteritis","Thromboangiitis obliterans"], key=4,
exp="Focal narrowings of the <b>aortic arch branches</b> in a <b>young woman</b> with arm claudication, unequal arm pressures, a weak radial pulse, bruits and a preceding systemic phase with a high ESR = <b>Takayasu arteritis (\"pulseless disease\")</b>, a large-vessel granulomatous vasculitis of the aorta and its main branches. Grossly the wall is massively thickened. Its histology looks like giant cell arteritis, so <b>age</b> separates them: Takayasu in young women, giant cell arteritis after 50.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Takayasu arteritis narrows the aortic arch branches in young women, causing pulseless arms, claudication, bruits and hypertension.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct with weak, delayed femoral pulses, higher arm than leg pressure and rib notching.",
1:"Would be correct for a string-of-beads lesion, usually in the renal or carotid arteries, with a normal ESR.",
2:"Would be correct in a patient older than 50 with temporal headache and jaw claudication.",
3:"Would be correct in an older smoker with vascular risk factors and a normal ESR.",
5:"Would be correct in a heavy smoker with distal limb ischemia, digital ulcers and migratory thrombophlebitis."},
eli5="Her immune system is inflaming the big arteries that come off the top of the aorta, squeezing them narrow. Her left arm gets too little blood, so its pulse is weak and it aches with use."),

dict(img='Vpanca', cap="Indirect immunofluorescence of the patient's serum on ethanol-fixed neutrophils",
stem="A 66-year-old man is admitted to the hospital because of a 1-week history of coughing up blood and a 3-week history of fatigue and dark urine. He has no history of asthma, chronic sinus symptoms, nasal crusting, or nosebleeds. His temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F), pulse is 104/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 152/90 mm Hg. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 89%. Crackles are heard over both lungs. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.8 g/dL, and the eosinophil count is normal. Serum creatinine concentration is 4.2 mg/dL; it was 0.9 mg/dL 2 months ago. Urinalysis shows red blood cell casts. CT of the chest shows diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities without nodules or cavities. A kidney biopsy specimen shows crescentic glomerulonephritis with few or no immune deposits on immunofluorescence. The result of indirect immunofluorescence testing of his serum is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Anti–glomerular basement membrane disease","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","IgA vasculitis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Polyarteritis nodosa"], key=4,
exp="Staining rimming the nucleus = <b>perinuclear ANCA (p-ANCA)</b>, which corresponds to anti-<b>myeloperoxidase (MPO)</b>. Pulmonary hemorrhage plus a pauci-immune (few deposits) crescentic glomerulonephritis, <b>without</b> sinus disease, nodules, cavities, asthma or eosinophilia = <b>microscopic polyangiitis (MPA)</b>: no granulomas, kidney + lung, p-ANCA in about 60%, often drug-associated. Mechanism: ANCA bind cytokine-primed neutrophils → neutrophil and complement activation → NETs (neutrophil extracellular traps) → endothelial damage.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Pulmonary-renal syndrome with p-ANCA (anti-MPO) and no granulomatous upper-airway disease is microscopic polyangiitis.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct with linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane and negative ANCA.",
1:"Would be correct with adult-onset asthma, eosinophilia and neuropathy.",
2:"Would be correct with chronic sinusitis, nasal crusting, cavitating nodules and a cytoplasmic (c-ANCA, anti-proteinase 3) pattern.",
3:"Would be correct with purpura, arthritis and abdominal pain, and IgA deposits on biopsy.",
5:"Would be correct for a medium-vessel, ANCA-negative vasculitis. PAN spares the lung."},
eli5="His blood carries antibodies that switch on white cells against the tiny vessels of his lungs and kidneys, so he coughs blood and his kidneys fail. The stain pattern around the nucleus points to this disease."),

dict(img='Vpurp', cap='Photograph of the right foot and ankle',
stem="A 56-year-old man comes to the office because of a 3-week history of a nonblanching rash on the feet and lower legs, aching in the knees and fingers, and burning numbness of both feet. He has had fatigue for several years. His temperature is 37.2°C (99.0°F), pulse is 80/min, and blood pressure is 142/88 mm Hg. The right foot is shown; the lesions are raised and palpable. The liver edge is palpable 2 cm below the costal margin. Serum alanine aminotransferase activity is 92 U/L. Platelet count is 228,000/mm³. Serum C3 concentration is normal, and C4 concentration is markedly decreased. Urinalysis shows 1+ protein and 10–20 red blood cells/hpf. Hepatitis C virus antibody test is positive, and hepatitis B surface antigen is negative. A skin biopsy specimen shows neutrophils fragmented around dermal venules.{FIG}Which of the following additional findings is most likely in this patient's serum?",
choices=["Immunoglobulins that precipitate at 4°C","IgA-containing circulating immune complexes","Antibodies to myeloperoxidase","Antibodies to proteinase 3","Antibodies to glomerular basement membrane"], key=0,
exp="Palpable purpura with leukocytoclastic small-vessel vasculitis, arthralgia, neuropathy, glomerulonephritis and <b>chronic hepatitis C</b> = <b>cryoglobulinemic vasculitis</b>, an immune-complex vasculitis. The culprit immunoglobulins are <b>cryoglobulins</b>, which <b>precipitate in the cold</b>. Dr. Gardner's list: hepatitis C and Sjögren syndrome. <i>Board add-on:</i> complement is consumed, with C4 characteristically very low.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Purpura, arthralgia, neuropathy and glomerulonephritis in hepatitis C point to cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, caused by immunoglobulins that precipitate in the cold.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for IgA vasculitis (Henoch–Schönlein purpura), in a child after an upper respiratory infection, without hepatitis C.",
2:"Would be correct for microscopic polyangiitis, a pauci-immune vasculitis with pulmonary hemorrhage and normal complement.",
3:"Would be correct for granulomatosis with polyangiitis, with sinus, lung and kidney disease.",
4:"Would be correct for anti-GBM disease, with lung hemorrhage and linear glomerular IgG, not purpura or neuropathy."},
eli5="Hepatitis C makes antibodies that clump together in cooler parts of the body like the legs. The clumps lodge in tiny vessels of the skin, joints, nerves and kidneys and inflame them."),

dict(img='Vleuko', cap='Photomicrograph of a skin biopsy specimen (H&E)',
stem="A 7-year-old boy is brought to the office because of a 4-day history of a rash on his legs and buttocks, pain in both knees, and intermittent cramping abdominal pain. Two weeks ago he had a sore throat and runny nose that resolved without treatment. His temperature is 37.4°C (99.3°F), pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. There are raised, nonblanching purple papules over the buttocks and the backs of the legs. Both knees are tender and mildly swollen. The abdomen is diffusely tender without guarding. Platelet count is 310,000/mm³; prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are normal. Urinalysis shows 2+ blood and 1+ protein. A photomicrograph of a biopsy specimen of one skin lesion is shown.{FIG}Direct immunofluorescence of this specimen is most likely to show which of the following?",
choices=["Granular IgA within small-vessel walls","Granular IgG and IgM in small-vessel walls","Linear IgG along basement membranes","Little or no immunoglobulin (pauci-immune)","Granular IgG and C3 at the dermal-epidermal junction"], key=0,
exp="Neutrophils blown apart into <b>nuclear dust</b> around small dermal vessels = <b>leukocytoclastic</b> (immune-complex) small-vessel vasculitis. A child <b>2 weeks after an upper respiratory infection</b> with the tetrad of palpable purpura, arthritis, abdominal pain and glomerulonephritis = <b>Henoch–Schönlein purpura (IgA vasculitis)</b> → <b>IgA immune complexes</b> in the vessel walls of skin, gut and glomeruli. The normal platelet count and clotting times show the purpura comes from vasculitis, not a bleeding disorder. In adults the same disease (IgA vasculitis) has worse kidney involvement.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Henoch–Schönlein purpura is a leukocytoclastic small-vessel vasculitis caused by IgA immune-complex deposition, typically in children after an upper respiratory infection.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for cryoglobulinemic vasculitis, typically an adult with hepatitis C and low C4.",
2:"Would be correct for anti-glomerular basement membrane disease, with pulmonary hemorrhage and glomerulonephritis but no purpura.",
3:"Would be correct for ANCA-associated vasculitis (granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis, or the eosinophilic form).",
4:"Would be correct for lupus skin (a positive lupus band test), with a photosensitive rash and positive antinuclear antibodies."},
eli5="After a cold, his body made IgA antibody clumps that got stuck in small blood vessels of his skin, joints, gut and kidneys. White cells attacking them burst apart, leaving the 'dust' you see."),

dict(img='Vathero', cap='Photomicrograph of a cross section of the proximal left anterior descending coronary artery (H&E)',
stem="A 58-year-old man dies suddenly while shoveling snow. He had hypertension, a serum LDL (low-density lipoprotein)-cholesterol concentration of 182 mg/dL, and a 35-pack-year history of cigarette smoking. At autopsy, the proximal left anterior descending coronary artery has the appearance shown.{FIG}The cells that synthesize most of the collagen in the fibrous cap of this lesion originate mainly from which of the following locations?",
choices=["Smooth muscle cells of the media","Fibroblasts of the adventitia","Circulating blood monocytes","Endothelial cells of the intima","Pericytes of the vasa vasorum","Platelets on the plaque surface"], key=0,
exp="An eccentric <b>atheroma</b>: a fibrous cap over a necrotic core (cholesterol clefts, debris, calcium), all within the <b>intima</b>. Response to injury: endothelial injury → LDL enters and is oxidized → monocytes become macrophages and foam cells → platelets adhere and release PDGF (platelet-derived growth factor) → <b>smooth muscle cells migrate in from the media</b>, proliferate and lay down collagen and matrix → the <b>fibrous cap</b>. Plaque components in one line: cells, matrix, lipids, calcium.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The fibrous cap of an atheroma is made mainly by smooth muscle cells that migrated from the media into the intima.",
wrong={1:"Adventitial fibroblasts build the outer collagen of the vessel. They don't move into the intima to form the cap.",
2:"Would be correct if the question asked for the origin of the foam cells and macrophages in the core.",
3:"Endothelial cells cover the plaque surface. When injured they start the process, but they don't make the cap's collagen.",
4:"Vasa vasorum supply the outer wall of large vessels. Their narrowing weakens the media in syphilitic aneurysms.",
5:"Platelets release PDGF that recruits smooth muscle cells, but they synthesize no collagen themselves."},
eli5="Muscle cells crawl in from the middle layer of the artery and patch over the fatty mess with a collagen lid. A thick lid is safer; a thin one can crack."),

dict(img='Vrupture', cap='Photomicrograph of a cross section of the proximal left anterior descending artery (arrow)',
stem="A 49-year-old man collapses at work and cannot be resuscitated. He had had no chest pain. Coronary angiography 2 years earlier, obtained for atypical chest pain, showed no narrowing greater than 40%. He smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily. At autopsy, there is no myocardial scar and no left ventricular thrombus. A cross section of the proximal left anterior descending artery is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of the luminal occlusion?",
choices=["Rupture of a thin fibrous cap over a lipid core","Slow growth of a calcified, collagen-rich plaque","Vasospasm of a normal coronary segment","Embolus from a left ventricular mural thrombus","Dissection of the media with an intramural hematoma","Endothelial erosion over a thick, fibrous plaque"], key=0,
exp="The lumen is filled by thrombus that connects through a <b>break in the plaque cap</b> (arrow) to the underlying core. A <b>thin-cap (vulnerable) plaque</b> ruptures or bleeds → the thrombogenic necrotic core is exposed to blood → an <b>acute occlusive thrombus</b> → myocardial infarction or <b>sudden cardiac death</b>. Dr. Gardner: \"forty years of slow injury, then a sudden event.\" A <b>thick-cap fibrocalcific</b> plaque instead narrows slowly to a critical stenosis and causes stable angina. <i>Board add-on:</i> culprit plaques are often only moderate stenoses beforehand, as here.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Sudden coronary occlusion usually comes from rupture of a thin-capped, lipid-rich plaque with superimposed thrombosis.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for gradually worsening stable angina from a fixed critical stenosis, with no acute thrombus.",
2:"Would be correct for variant (Prinzmetal) angina with transient ST elevation and a normal artery.",
3:"Ruled out by the absence of a scar or left ventricular thrombus. An embolus would come from an old infarct or atrial fibrillation.",
4:"Would be correct for spontaneous coronary artery dissection, with blood inside the media rather than a plaque break.",
5:"Erosion leaves the cap intact under the thrombus. Here the cap is broken and the thrombus extends into the core."},
eli5="A fatty bump in his heart artery had only a thin skin over it. The skin cracked, a clot formed on the crack in minutes, and the artery closed."),

dict(img='Vaaa', cap='Gross specimen of the abdominal aorta and kidneys (asterisk)',
stem="A 76-year-old man dies of a myocardial infarction. He had hypertension and a 60-pack-year history of cigarette smoking. He was 175 cm (5 ft 9 in) tall with normal body proportions and had no history of trauma, fever, or bacteremia. Serologic tests for syphilis 1 year earlier were negative. His abdominal aorta and kidneys, removed at autopsy, are shown; the lesion is marked with an asterisk.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely primary cause of this lesion?",
choices=["Atherosclerotic weakening of the wall","Fibrillin-1 defect with medial degeneration","Infection of the wall by circulating bacteria","Endarteritis of the vasa vasorum from syphilis","Traumatic tear contained by adventitia","Type III collagen defect in the wall"], key=0,
exp="A fusiform dilation of the aorta <b>below the renal arteries</b>, with yellow plaque, in an elderly smoker with hypertension = <b>abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)</b>, the <b>most common</b> aneurysm. It usually sits below the renal arteries and often runs into the iliac bifurcation, and it is driven by <b>atherosclerosis</b> (risk factors: smoking, hypertension, age). Plaque inflammation and matrix metalloproteinases break down the media → the wall gives way. <i>Board add-on:</i> one-time ultrasound screening for men aged 65–75 who have ever smoked.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm is the most common aneurysm and is caused mainly by atherosclerosis in older smokers with hypertension.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for Marfan syndrome, in a tall young patient with long limbs and lens dislocation. It affects the ascending aorta.",
2:"Would be correct for a mycotic aneurysm after bacteremia or endocarditis.",
3:"Would be correct with positive syphilis serology. Syphilis targets the ascending (thoracic) aorta.",
4:"Would be correct for a false aneurysm (pseudoaneurysm) after trauma or catheter puncture.",
5:"Would be correct for vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome, with thin skin and rupture of medium arteries in a young patient."},
eli5="Decades of smoking and high blood pressure clogged and weakened the lower aorta, and it slowly ballooned out below the kidney arteries."),

dict(img='Vdouble', cap='Contrast-enhanced CT of the chest at the level of the pulmonary artery (arrow)',
stem="A 62-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 1 hour after the sudden onset of severe, tearing pain between the shoulder blades that has moved toward the lower back. He has hypertension treated with amlodipine but often misses doses. His pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 196/108 mm Hg in the right arm and 190/104 mm Hg in the left arm. No heart murmur is heard. Femoral and pedal pulses are palpable bilaterally. Urine output is adequate, and serum creatinine and lactate concentrations are within the reference ranges. An ECG shows sinus tachycardia without ST-segment changes. A CT scan of the chest is shown; the ascending aorta and arch appear normal on all images.{FIG}In addition to admission to the intensive care unit, which of the following is the most appropriate management?",
choices=["Emergency open surgical replacement of the aorta","Intravenous esmolol, then a vasodilator if needed","Intravenous alteplase followed by a heparin infusion","Emergency coronary angiography with stenting","Intravenous hydralazine as the first and only agent"], key=1,
exp="Tearing interscapular pain moving downward plus a CT showing an <b>intimal flap</b> with true and false lumens in the <b>descending</b> aorta only (the ascending aorta is normal) = <b>DeBakey III / Stanford type B aortic dissection</b>. Dr. Gardner: type B is <b>often managed conservatively with blood pressure control</b>; types I/II (Stanford A, ascending aorta involved) go to <b>surgery</b>. No malperfusion here (pulses, urine output and lactate are normal). <i>Board add-on:</i> give an intravenous β-blocker first to lower heart rate and the force of ejection, then add a vasodilator; intervene if malperfusion, rupture or uncontrolled pain develops.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Uncomplicated Stanford type B (descending-only) aortic dissection is treated medically with β-blockade and blood pressure control; type A needs emergency surgery.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct if the flap involved the ascending aorta (Stanford A), or with rupture or malperfusion.",
2:"Thrombolysis would be correct for some ST-elevation infarctions or massive pulmonary emboli. In a dissection it can cause fatal bleeding.",
3:"Would be correct with ST elevation from a coronary occlusion. The ECG shows no ST changes.",
4:"A vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia, which raises shear on the aortic wall. Heart-rate control must come first."},
eli5="A tear let blood split the wall of the aorta in his back, but the part next to the heart is fine. Lowering the heart rate and pressure stops the split from spreading, and he may not need surgery."),

dict(img='Veds', cap="Photograph of the patient's face",
stem="A 27-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 40 minutes after the sudden onset of severe left upper quadrant pain while lifting a box. Her pulse is 128/min, and blood pressure is 82/50 mm Hg. CT angiography shows hemoperitoneum from rupture of the splenic artery. She bruises easily, and at age 22 she had a spontaneous perforation of the sigmoid colon. Her brother died suddenly at age 31 of a ruptured artery. She is 160 cm (5 ft 3 in) tall, and her arm span equals her height. Her lenses are in normal position. Her joints have normal range of motion, and her skin is thin, with visible veins over the chest, but it is not hyperextensible. Her face is shown.{FIG}A defect in which of the following proteins is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?",
choices=["Elastin","Fibrillin-1","Type I collagen","Type III collagen","Type V collagen"], key=3,
exp="Spontaneous rupture of a <b>medium-sized artery</b> and of the bowel, easy bruising, thin skin with visible veins, a thin face with prominent eyes and sunken cheeks, and a family history of arterial rupture = <b>vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome (type IV)</b> → a defect in <b>type III collagen</b> (α1 chain). Dr. Gardner's comparison table: vascular EDS carries a <b>high risk of arterial rupture of medium arteries</b>, whereas Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1) causes aortic root dilation and type A dissection.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Vascular Ehlers–Danlos syndrome is caused by a type III collagen defect and presents with spontaneous rupture of medium arteries, bowel or uterus.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for Williams syndrome (supravalvular aortic stenosis) or cutis laxa (loose, sagging skin). Neither causes spontaneous artery rupture.",
1:"Would be correct for Marfan syndrome: tall, arm span greater than height, upward lens dislocation, aortic root dilation.",
2:"Would be correct for osteogenesis imperfecta: fractures with minimal trauma, blue sclerae, hearing loss.",
4:"Would be correct for classic Ehlers–Danlos syndrome: hyperextensible skin and hypermobile joints, rarely arterial rupture."},
eli5="The collagen that should make her arteries and gut tough is faulty, so they can tear on their own. Her thin skin and face are outside clues to the same weak protein."),

dict(img='Vks', cap='Photograph of the arm',
stem="A 36-year-old man comes to the office because of a 2-month history of painless purple lesions on the arms and legs that have slowly enlarged. He has HIV infection and stopped taking antiretroviral therapy 2 years ago. His temperature is 37.0°C (98.6°F). The lesions on his arm are shown; similar lesions are present on the hard palate. His CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is 60/mm³. A Warthin-Starry silver stain of a biopsy specimen of one lesion shows no organisms, and the specimen contains no neutrophilic infiltrate.{FIG}Which of the following infectious agents is the most likely cause of these lesions?",
choices=["Bartonella henselae","Cytomegalovirus","Epstein-Barr virus","Human herpesvirus 8","Human papillomavirus"], key=3,
exp="Violaceous plaques and nodules of the skin and palate in a man with advanced HIV infection = <b>Kaposi sarcoma</b>, an indolent vascular tumor caused by <b>human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)</b>. Histology: spindle cells forming slit-like vascular spaces filled with red cells. Dr. Gardner adds the sporadic form in older Mediterranean men. Its look-alike is <b>bacillary angiomatosis</b>, a reactive, infection-induced vascular proliferation; the negative silver stain and the absence of neutrophils remove it.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Purple skin and mucosal plaques in advanced HIV infection are Kaposi sarcoma, caused by human herpesvirus 8.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for bacillary angiomatosis, with silver-stained bacilli and neutrophils in the lesion; it responds to antibiotics.",
1:"Would be correct for retinitis, colitis or esophagitis in advanced HIV, not violaceous vascular plaques.",
2:"Would be correct for oral hairy leukoplakia or primary CNS lymphoma in HIV infection.",
4:"Would be correct for warts, condylomas or cervical and anal carcinoma."},
eli5="A herpes-family virus makes blood-vessel cells grow into purple patches when the immune system is too weak to control it."),

dict(img='Vstraw', cap='Photograph of the forehead',
stem="A 4-month-old girl is brought for a well-child examination. Her parents first noticed a faint red mark on her forehead at 2 weeks of age; it grew rapidly over the next 3 months and has not changed in size during the past 3 weeks. She was born at term and is feeding and growing well. The lesion is shown; it is soft, compressible, and not ulcerated, and it does not obstruct either eyelid. There are no other skin lesions, and the neurologic examination shows no abnormalities. Platelet count is 290,000/mm³.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely course of this lesion if it is left untreated?",
choices=["Gradual regression over the first years of childhood","Growth in proportion to the child, lasting for life","Transformation into angiosarcoma in adolescence","Enlargement with consumption of platelets and fibrinogen","Development of leptomeningeal vascular malformations"], key=0,
exp="A bright red, raised, lobulated lesion that appeared in early infancy, grew fast and has stopped growing = a <b>juvenile \"strawberry\" (capillary) hemangioma</b>. Dr. Gardner: it looks surprising but <b>regresses spontaneously</b> during the first decade, often leaving no trace. <i>Board add-on:</i> these lesions grow for months, plateau, then involute; propranolol is reserved for lesions that threaten vision, the airway or ulcerate.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Juvenile (strawberry) capillary hemangiomas appear in infancy, grow, then regress spontaneously in childhood.",
wrong={1:"Would be correct for a port-wine stain (nevus flammeus): a flat, purple vascular malformation present at birth that never regresses.",
2:"Hemangiomas do not turn malignant. Angiosarcoma is a rare adult tumor linked historically to vinyl chloride.",
3:"Would be correct for Kasabach–Merritt phenomenon, with a falling platelet count, which arises in rarer vascular tumors.",
4:"Would be correct for Sturge–Weber syndrome, with a facial port-wine stain in the trigeminal distribution and seizures."},
eli5="This is a baby birthmark made of extra tiny blood vessels. It grows for a few months, then slowly fades over the next few years, usually on its own."),

dict(img='Vraynaud', cap='Photograph of the right hand during an episode',
stem="A 46-year-old woman comes to the office because of a 1-year history of episodes in which her fingers turn white and then blue when she is cold. The episodes have become more frequent and more severe and now affect the right hand more than the left. The right hand during an episode is shown. Over the past 6 months she has had heartburn and difficulty swallowing solid food, and the skin of her fingers has become tight. She has never smoked. She does not have purpura, and she has never had hepatitis. Symptoms do not change with arm position. There are two small pitted scars on the fingertips. Radial and ulnar pulses are 2+ bilaterally.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's episodes?",
choices=["Atherosclerotic peripheral artery disease","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Primary Raynaud phenomenon","Systemic sclerosis","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Thromboangiitis obliterans"], key=3,
exp="A sharply blanched finger = <b>Raynaud phenomenon</b> (exaggerated arterial and arteriolar vasoconstriction to cold or emotion). Here it is <b>asymmetric</b> and <b>progressively worse</b>, began at 45, and comes with fingertip pits, tight skin and dysphagia → <b>secondary Raynaud</b> from an autoimmune disease, here <b>systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)</b>. Primary Raynaud (3–5% of people, young women) is symmetric and stable → reassure. About 10% of diagnosed Raynaud turns out to have an autoimmune cause.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Asymmetric, progressive Raynaud phenomenon with digital pitting, tight skin or dysphagia is secondary Raynaud, often from systemic sclerosis or lupus.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct with diminished pulses and vascular risk factors in an older smoker.",
1:"Would be correct with palpable purpura, hepatitis C and low C4.",
2:"Would be correct for symmetric, stable attacks in a young woman with no tissue damage or systemic symptoms.",
4:"Would be correct if symptoms changed with arm position.",
5:"Would be correct in a heavy smoker with distal ischemia and migratory thrombophlebitis. She has never smoked."},
eli5="Her finger arteries clamp down too hard in the cold. Because it is getting worse, is lopsided, and comes with tight skin and trouble swallowing, it points to scleroderma rather than harmless Raynaud."),
]
